WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.251 --> 00:00:07.754
Has it ever occurred to you,
as you walk down the street,

00:00:07.796 --> 00:00:11.424
just how much data is flowing
beneath your feet?

00:00:12.175 --> 00:00:15.095
A wealth of information
on our health and our well-being

00:00:15.136 --> 00:00:17.430
is running through our city sewers,

00:00:17.472 --> 00:00:21.351
and we're all contributing to it
every single time we use the toilet.

00:00:22.561 --> 00:00:23.728
Think about it.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.231
Everybody pees and poops,

00:00:26.273 --> 00:00:30.151
and we know that urine and stool
contain a rich source of information

00:00:30.151 --> 00:00:31.736
on our health and our well-being.

00:00:32.195 --> 00:00:36.783
Our doctors look at it all the time
to analyze for a variety of things.

00:00:37.158 --> 00:00:38.785
Now, every time you flush,

00:00:38.785 --> 00:00:42.539
you're sending this valuable information
down into our sewers,

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:46.376
where it's mixing with waste from hundreds
of thousands of other people.

00:00:46.418 --> 00:00:49.379
Once collected, it looks
something like this.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:51.423
This tiny sample

00:00:51.423 --> 00:00:53.466
comes from a wastewater treatment plant

00:00:53.508 --> 00:00:56.219
that represents more
than one million people.

00:00:56.219 --> 00:01:00.390
And from it, we can detect all sorts
of things about that community:

00:01:01.141 --> 00:01:05.562
the infectious disease viruses
that are circulating in our bodies,

00:01:05.562 --> 00:01:09.733
chemical markers for the drugs
that are most commonly consumed.

00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:14.863
And we can analyze for all the bacteria
that live in our collective microbiomes.

00:01:15.488 --> 00:01:17.532
Now, if this sounds too close for comfort,

00:01:17.532 --> 00:01:21.286
just consider all the personalized data
that you're parting with every day

00:01:21.286 --> 00:01:24.914
when you use gadgets like
your smartphone or your smart watch.

00:01:24.956 --> 00:01:27.042
What's amazing about sewage

00:01:27.042 --> 00:01:30.211
is that it's naturally
aggregated and anonymized.

00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:32.297
Once flushed,

00:01:32.339 --> 00:01:35.717
your waste is mixing with that
of thousands and thousands of people,

00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:39.095
so there's actually no way
to tie any information from here

00:01:39.095 --> 00:01:40.972
back to a specific person.

00:01:41.264 --> 00:01:44.643
Put differently,
it's the perfect data dump.

00:01:44.684 --> 00:01:47.187
(Laughter)

00:01:47.187 --> 00:01:50.190
The thoughtful collection
and analysis of sewage

00:01:50.231 --> 00:01:53.401
has the potential to radically
improve health outcomes

00:01:53.401 --> 00:01:55.278
in cities around the world,

00:01:55.278 --> 00:01:58.990
and it's a growing field
called "wastewater epidemiology."

00:01:59.324 --> 00:02:02.077
And wastewater epidemiology
is but one example

00:02:02.118 --> 00:02:05.997
of all the big data that we're
generating in our cities today.

00:02:07.082 --> 00:02:11.086
Consider all the data that you generate
with every phone call, package delivered,

00:02:11.086 --> 00:02:12.504
mile driven.

00:02:12.837 --> 00:02:16.007
It's data from cameras, sensors, drones,

00:02:16.007 --> 00:02:18.760
air quality, water quality monitoring,

00:02:18.802 --> 00:02:22.263
and the vast amounts of information
generated by our health care

00:02:22.263 --> 00:02:24.224
and our educational systems.

00:02:24.891 --> 00:02:28.103
All of this information,
these digital breadcrumbs,

00:02:28.103 --> 00:02:32.732
tell us unique stories about our cities
and the way that we live our lives.

00:02:33.149 --> 00:02:37.445
The thoughtful collection
and analysis of this information

00:02:37.445 --> 00:02:40.782
has the power to inform
real-time improvements

00:02:40.824 --> 00:02:42.659
to things like social policy,

00:02:42.701 --> 00:02:45.370
environmental management,
health equity and more.

00:02:45.745 --> 00:02:48.707
As an architect, I believe
that we need to harness

00:02:48.707 --> 00:02:51.584
the hundreds of millions
of terabytes of data

00:02:51.626 --> 00:02:54.671
that we're generating in our cities
each and every day.

00:02:54.671 --> 00:02:57.048
And this is important now more than ever,

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.592
because for the first time
in human history,

00:02:59.592 --> 00:03:02.971
more than half of all people
live in cities.

00:03:02.971 --> 00:03:04.264
By 2050,

00:03:04.264 --> 00:03:07.892
this number will grow
to nearly seven in 10 people.

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:10.895
Now just think about what
that means for a second.

00:03:10.937 --> 00:03:12.897
It means our biggest crises,

00:03:12.939 --> 00:03:16.860
from climate change to pandemics
to growing inequality,

00:03:16.901 --> 00:03:19.904
are going to hit cities first and hardest.

00:03:21.072 --> 00:03:24.033
But the era of big data
offers an opportunity

00:03:24.075 --> 00:03:27.412
for new and creative solutions
to tackle these problems.

00:03:29.038 --> 00:03:33.126
So let's dive into the opportunity
presented by wastewater epidemiology.

00:03:34.335 --> 00:03:37.505
Some of you may have heard of it
as it gained a lot of popularity

00:03:37.505 --> 00:03:40.300
and attention during
the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:03:41.009 --> 00:03:44.512
In 2020, research groups
from around the world

00:03:44.554 --> 00:03:47.390
began detecting SARS-CoV-2 RNA,

00:03:47.432 --> 00:03:50.518
the virus that causes COVID-19,
in sewage samples.

00:03:51.102 --> 00:03:53.021
I was on one of those teams.

00:03:53.605 --> 00:03:57.150
We and others showed
that you can actually use sewage

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:00.653
as an accurate representation
of COVID activity in our communities.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:02.572
Let me show you what I mean.

00:04:02.572 --> 00:04:06.618
Here we're looking at a time series
over the course of the pandemic.

00:04:06.951 --> 00:04:10.079
So from March 2020 through just last week.

00:04:10.079 --> 00:04:15.001
The blue line represents COVID virus
concentrations in sewage samples

00:04:15.043 --> 00:04:16.920
from across the United States.

00:04:16.961 --> 00:04:20.548
In yellow, we see COVID
clinical case data.

00:04:21.132 --> 00:04:25.053
For the first two years of the pandemic,
case data was very reliable.

00:04:25.053 --> 00:04:27.889
People were getting
PCR-tested all the time.

00:04:27.889 --> 00:04:29.098
During those two years,

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.100
the two data sets tracked very well.

00:04:31.142 --> 00:04:32.310
That was great.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:34.479
It meant that sewage was also reliable

00:04:34.521 --> 00:04:37.106
and an accurate representation
of disease burden.

00:04:37.857 --> 00:04:40.693
However, over the past
year and a half to two years,

00:04:40.693 --> 00:04:43.613
we've seen a divergence
in those data sets.

00:04:43.613 --> 00:04:46.825
People just aren't getting
COVID-tested nearly as often.

00:04:46.825 --> 00:04:48.743
Sewage, on the other hand,

00:04:48.785 --> 00:04:52.163
doesn't require us
to access health care services.

00:04:52.163 --> 00:04:55.834
We're all represented just
by peeing and pooping.

00:04:55.834 --> 00:05:00.129
Throughout the pandemic, we and others
also showed that sewage is predictive

00:05:00.129 --> 00:05:04.592
and a leading indicator
of new COVID clinical cases.

00:05:04.634 --> 00:05:08.763
This is because infectious disease
viruses incubate in our bodies

00:05:08.763 --> 00:05:11.891
before we develop symptoms
or go get tested.

00:05:12.308 --> 00:05:15.603
Meanwhile, we've been excreting
the virus for days.

00:05:16.271 --> 00:05:17.438
During COVID,

00:05:17.438 --> 00:05:21.568
it was shown that sewage
was anywhere between one to three weeks

00:05:21.568 --> 00:05:24.028
leading indicator for clinical cases.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.031
Now I'm going to show you an example

00:05:27.073 --> 00:05:30.702
of one time that this led
to a big community-impacting decision.

00:05:31.244 --> 00:05:34.497
Here, we're looking at data
from the Boston area

00:05:34.539 --> 00:05:36.332
during the Omicron wave.

00:05:36.332 --> 00:05:39.460
In December 2021,
towards the end of the month,

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.505
COVID cases began to skyrocket
across the country

00:05:42.547 --> 00:05:45.341
and didn't slow until the end of January.

00:05:45.341 --> 00:05:47.760
Boston Children's Hospital,
though, was ready.

00:05:47.802 --> 00:05:50.013
They had been looking
at Boston area sewage

00:05:50.054 --> 00:05:53.766
and saw the sewage levels
go up weeks earlier,

00:05:53.808 --> 00:05:58.479
so they proactively postponed
all non-emergency medical procedures.

00:05:59.522 --> 00:06:03.401
They wanted to free up resources
so that they could adequately respond

00:06:03.443 --> 00:06:05.862
to the incoming wave of hospitalizations.

00:06:07.238 --> 00:06:09.657
Now wastewater epidemiology has been used

00:06:09.657 --> 00:06:12.535
to tackle other pressing
health issues as well.

00:06:12.994 --> 00:06:14.203
Before the pandemic,

00:06:14.203 --> 00:06:17.123
the biggest public health crisis
in the United States

00:06:17.123 --> 00:06:19.334
was our growing drug epidemic.

00:06:20.001 --> 00:06:22.754
Drug overdoses were growing year over year

00:06:22.795 --> 00:06:26.049
and had become the leading
cause of accidental death

00:06:26.049 --> 00:06:28.593
for Americans under the age of 50.

00:06:28.927 --> 00:06:34.098
In 2018, a small town in North Carolina
had seen overdoses go up,

00:06:34.098 --> 00:06:36.059
and they wanted better information,

00:06:36.059 --> 00:06:38.144
better data to know what to do about it,

00:06:38.186 --> 00:06:41.064
what was driving this trend
and how to respond.

00:06:41.064 --> 00:06:45.151
So we turned to the sewers,
and together with the mayor's office,

00:06:45.193 --> 00:06:50.031
we began to analyze sewage samples
from several sites across the city

00:06:50.073 --> 00:06:53.952
and were able to show
that prescription opioids

00:06:53.952 --> 00:06:57.747
were the drug most commonly consumed,
not injectable opioids.

00:06:58.748 --> 00:07:00.667
Equipped with this data,

00:07:00.667 --> 00:07:04.087
the city diverted resources
from needle exchange sites

00:07:04.128 --> 00:07:07.757
and put that money into medication
takeback programs instead.

00:07:08.174 --> 00:07:10.677
They advertised and held
dozens of town halls

00:07:10.718 --> 00:07:14.722
where they talked about the adverse
effects of prescription painkillers.

00:07:15.264 --> 00:07:16.724
That year,

00:07:16.766 --> 00:07:21.396
the city saw a 40 percent
reduction in overdoses,

00:07:21.437 --> 00:07:22.730
and for the first time,

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:26.192
they had engaged their community
in a dialogue around drugs,

00:07:26.234 --> 00:07:28.236
addiction and overdose.

00:07:28.736 --> 00:07:34.450
Now imagine if every city around the world
had access to this sort of information.

00:07:34.951 --> 00:07:36.119
Before the pandemic,

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:39.080
wastewater epidemiology was a tiny field

00:07:39.122 --> 00:07:42.333
with no more than a dozen
experts worldwide.

00:07:42.333 --> 00:07:45.753
Today, 72 countries

00:07:45.753 --> 00:07:50.008
have used wastewater monitoring
to understand COVID-19.

00:07:50.675 --> 00:07:53.094
And it's time that we leverage
these investments

00:07:53.094 --> 00:07:55.596
to monitor for all sorts
of other things as well.

00:07:56.305 --> 00:08:00.810
Imagine knowing when influenza
and RSV are going to peak every year

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:02.854
so that our hospitals can prepare.

00:08:03.229 --> 00:08:05.898
Imagine mapping nutrition in our cities

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:08.109
so that we can identify food deserts

00:08:08.109 --> 00:08:10.528
and understand social
determinants of health.

00:08:11.154 --> 00:08:15.867
Imagine identifying superbugs
and antibiotic resistant genes

00:08:15.908 --> 00:08:17.910
as they emerge in our communities.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.623
Imagine preventing the next pandemic
before it happens.

00:08:23.499 --> 00:08:28.504
In the way that cholera prompted London
to build modern-day sewer systems,

00:08:28.546 --> 00:08:31.049
and poor health in the tenements
of New York City

00:08:31.049 --> 00:08:35.094
were one of the catalysts behind
the building of Central Park,

00:08:35.136 --> 00:08:38.306
this is how our cities
can learn from COVID-19.

00:08:38.806 --> 00:08:44.395
And this is precisely how we can foster
a new, intelligent kind of urbanization.

00:08:45.354 --> 00:08:48.566
For years now, scientists, policymakers,

00:08:48.566 --> 00:08:50.735
architects and urban planners

00:08:50.735 --> 00:08:54.155
have been harnessing the power
of technology and big data

00:08:54.197 --> 00:08:56.282
to future-proof our cities.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:58.743
Over the last decade,

00:08:58.743 --> 00:09:01.913
chief technology officers
have been appointed in cities

00:09:01.954 --> 00:09:03.456
around the world.

00:09:04.415 --> 00:09:06.542
Roles once reserved for the boardrooms

00:09:06.584 --> 00:09:08.044
and hallways of Silicon Valley

00:09:08.044 --> 00:09:10.838
are now finally open in city hall.

00:09:12.006 --> 00:09:15.134
So next time you swipe your credit card,

00:09:15.176 --> 00:09:18.638
take a ride in a taxi
or tap your MetroCard,

00:09:18.679 --> 00:09:20.640
just consider how you're contributing

00:09:20.681 --> 00:09:23.559
to your city's ever-growing
digital infrastructure.

00:09:24.685 --> 00:09:27.480
And next time you use the toilet,

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:30.608
just remember, you're doing
your civic duty.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:32.193
(Laughter)

00:09:32.235 --> 00:09:33.402
Thank you.

00:09:33.402 --> 00:09:37.323
(Applause)

